Database Design – Real-Time Chat System
1. Overview

The database is designed to support a highly scalable, low-latency messaging system.
A NoSQL database (e.g., MongoDB / Cassandra) is preferred due to:

High write throughput
Horizontal scalability
Flexible schema for messages
2. Design Approach
Denormalized schema for faster reads
Optimized for write-heavy workloads
Partitioning based on chat_id or user_id
Indexing for efficient querying
3. Collections / Tables
3.1 Users Collection
{
  "user_id": "u123",
  "name": "Ayush Rana",
  "phone": "9876543210",
  "profile_pic": "url",
  "status": "online",
  "last_seen": "timestamp",
  "created_at": "timestamp"
}
Indexes:
user_id (Primary Key)
phone (Unique)
3.2 Chats Collection
{
  "chat_id": "c123",
  "type": "direct", 
  "participants": ["u123", "u456"],
  "created_at": "timestamp",
  "last_message": {
    "message_id": "m123",
    "content": "Hello",
    "timestamp": "timestamp"
  }
}
Indexes:
chat_id (Primary Key)
participants (for quick lookup of user chats)
3.3 Messages Collection
{
  "message_id": "m123",
  "chat_id": "c123",
  "sender_id": "u123",
  "type": "text",
  "content": "Hello",
  "media_url": null,
  "timestamp": "timestamp",
  "status": "sent",
  "client_msg_id": "temp_001"
}
Indexes:
message_id (Primary Key)
chat_id + timestamp (for message retrieval)
client_msg_id (for idempotency)
3.4 Group Members Collection (For Group Chats)
{
  "chat_id": "c123",
  "user_id": "u456",
  "role": "member",
  "joined_at": "timestamp"
}
Indexes:
chat_id
user_id
3.5 Message Status Collection (Read Receipts)
{
  "message_id": "m123",
  "user_id": "u456",
  "status": "read",
  "timestamp": "timestamp"
}
Indexes:
message_id
user_id
4. Relationships (Logical)
One user → multiple chats
One chat → multiple messages
One message → multiple status entries (for group read receipts)
5. Data Partitioning (Sharding)
Shard Key Options:
chat_id → best for message distribution
user_id → best for user-centric queries

 Shard on chat_id
Reason: Messages are fetched chat-wise

6. Caching Strategy
Use Redis for:
Recent messages
Active chats
Online users
7. Storage Optimization
Store media files in Object Storage (e.g., S3)
Save only media_url in database
Compress old messages (cold storage)
8. Consistency Considerations
Eventual consistency is acceptable
Strong consistency only for:
Message ordering within a chat
Delivery acknowledgment
9. Scalability Considerations
Horizontal scaling using sharding
Separate read/write replicas
Batch writes for message storage
